"agmai.org (https://agmai.org/)\nciteturn1000view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn999view0\",\"id\":7}); Total lines: 43\nL0: ### Advisory Group on Mathematics and Artificial Intelligence\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Institute for Advanced Study†www.ias.edu L3: \nL4: * * *\nL5: \nL6: The rapid advances in artificial intelligence (AI) present both opportunities and challenges for mathematical research. We believe that we are at a historic moment for our discipline. Recent events raise urgent questions about how to support the long-term prospects for deep human understanding of mathematics.\nL7: ## Purpose\nL8: \nL9: The purpose of this group is to advise AI companies on their interactions with mathematical research and with the mathematical community, including the responsible presentation and release of mathematical results. We seek to work for the best interest of mathematics and the mathematical community, and to serve as one possible channel of communication between mathematicians and the AI industry.\nL10: ## Independence, Transparency, and Accountability\nL11: This group operates independently of any AI company and members do not accept payment for this work. We will publish our recommendations to AI companies on this website. We are willing to offer such recommendations to any AI company whose models are likely to have a significant impact on mathematics. Although we will give advice, we do not have decision making power at any AI company, and the responsibility for the decisions made by any company will rest with that company.\nL12: ## Advisory Group Members\nL13: \nL14: • François Charles (ENS-PSL)\nL15: • Camillo De Lellis (IAS, GSSI)\nL16: • Timothy Gowers (Collège de France, Cambridge)\nL17: • Martin Hairer (EPFL, Imperial College London)\nL18: • Nikhil Srivastava (Berkeley, Simons Institute)\nL19: • Ulrike Tillmann (Oxford, INI)\nL20: • Ravi Vakil (Stanford)\nL21: • Edward Witten (IAS)\nL22: • Melanie Matchett Wood (Harvard)\nL23: This group came together after OpenAI approached some of its members about establishing an external advisory board. In agreement with OpenAI, they decided to create an independent group and invite others to join.\nL24: \nL25: * * *\nL26: ## Current Task\nL27: \nL28: We are currently facing the very specific challenge of advising OpenAI on how to coordinate the release of a large number of significant results in mathematics that they report have been produced by their internal model.\nL29: \nL30: We welcome input from the mathematical community on this question. Please use cite1†this form to share your thoughts with us as soon as possible. Your responses will be used to inform our recommendations and will not be made public without your approval.\nL31: \nL32: * * *\nL33: email: contact@agmai.org\nL34: \nL35:   *     * cite2†agmai.org L36:     * cite3†Sign up†wordpress.com L37:     * cite4†Log in†wordpress.com L38:     * cite5†Copy shortlink†wp.me L39:     * cite6†Report this content†wordpress.com L40:     * cite7†Manage subscriptions†subscribe.wordpress.com L41: \nL42: cite8†Image†pixel.wp.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI Academy (https://academy.openai.com/public/courses/ai-foundations-dnq5w)\nciteturn1000view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn999view1\",\"id\":18}); Total lines: 13\nL0: OpenAI Academy\nL1: \nL2: Search[Input: Search]\nL3: \nL4: Sign in\nL5: \nL6: cite0†Terms of Use†www.openai.com L7: \nL8: cite1†Privacy Policy†openai.com L9: \nL10: cite2†Code of Conduct L11: \nL12: Your Privacy Choices\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn Australian Youth Safety Blueprint (https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/7e70543d-a803-44b7-8754-ca00b3f2ff0c/australian-youth-safety-blueprint.pdf)\nciteturn1000view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 7; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn999view2\",\"id\":4}); Total lines: 219\nL0@P0: An Australian Youth Safety Blueprint\nL1@P0: September 2026\nL2@P0: Foreword\nL3@P0: Today’s young people will be the first generation to grow up with AI as part of everyday life. Nearly\nL4@P0: nine in ten teens who use ChatGPT turn to it for learning, information, skill-building, or productivity\nL5@P0: in a given week. That opportunity comes with a clear responsibility: teens should have experiences\nL6@P0: designed around their distinct developmental needs, with strong protections by default and\nL7@P0: meaningful opportunities to learn, create, build skills, and prepare for the future. Getting this right\nL8@P0: matters.\nL9@P0: Unlike with earlier technological shifts, particularly social media, we have an opportunity to put\nL10@P0: meaningful protections in place from the outset. The responsibility should not fall primarily on young\nL11@P0: people or their families: companies must build age-appropriate safeguards into their products by\nL12@P0: default, while giving parents and guardians accessible tools and clear information.\nL13@P0: As teenagers embrace AI for learning and creativity, Australia’s existing and proposed\nL14@P0: frameworks—including Australia’s proposed Digital Duty of Care reforms, currently under\nL15@P0: consideration in the recently released draft of the Online Safety Amendment (Digital Duty of Care)\nL16@P0: Bill 2026 —should provide targeted, harmonised protections that reflect the distinct characteristics\nL17@P0: of AI while preserving young people’s access to beneficial tools. Just as access to books unlocks\nL18@P0: the value of a library, access to safe AI unlocks new opportunities for personalised learning, helps\nL19@P0-1: reduce barriers for the underserved, and expands economic opportunity.Around the world, governments, educators, researchers, civil society, and industry are working to\nL20@P1: expand safe access, study real-world outcomes, and develop trusted evidence and practical\nL21@P1: guidance. Australia can help lead that effort by advancing standards that protect young people\nL22@P1: while promoting AI literacy, learning, creativity, and future opportunity.\nL23@P1: At OpenAI, four commitments guide our work for teens\nL24@P1: ● Put teen safety first: We’ve built age-appropriate safeguards into the ChatGPT\nL25@P1: experience.\nL26@P1: ● Encourage access to real-world support: Reinforce trusted relationships and\nL27@P1: encourage support from people who are in the teen’s life.\nL28@P1: ● Treat teens like teens: Reflect their developmental stage without talking down to them, or\nL29@P1: treating them like adults.\nL30@P1: ● Be transparent: Set clear expectations about how our systems work and how they should\nL31@P1: behave.\nL32@P1: OpenAI has put these commitments into practice through our products, global policy principles,\nL33@P1: and leadership on advancing strong legislative frameworks, like the Parents & Kids Safe AI Act. In\nL34@P1: ChatGPT, that includes parental controls to help families guide their teens’ use, Under-18 Principles\nL35@P1: in our Model Spec (as outlined above), and age prediction designed to apply the right protections\nL36@P1: to the right users. In August we began rolling out ChatGPT for Teens, the new default experience\nL37@P1: for users identified as ages 13 through 17 in Australia. This experience builds on OpenAI’s existing\nL38@P1: efforts to help young people learn, grow, and develop study skills, with updated guardrails\nL39@P1: designed to provide a safer environment.\nL40@P1: Youth AI safety requires sustained international cooperation, trusted evidence, and practical\nL41@P1: guidance that can keep pace with the technology. Governments, researchers, civil society,\nL42@P1: educators, and industry should work together to share evidence, develop common standards, and\nL43@P1: translate shared goals into effective safeguards. Australia can play a leading role in that effort,\nL44@P1: helping raise youth AI safety standards across the region and globally.\nL45@P1: This Australian Youth Safety Blueprint is intended to support that leadership. It sets out core\nL46@P1: principles for protections that are evidence-based, tailored to AI, feasible to implement, and\nL47@P1: focused on meaningful safety outcomes. We hope it provides a practical foundation for an\nL48@P1: Australian regulatory approach that is ambitious in protecting young people, clear in its\nL49@P1: expectations, and capable of supporting innovation that benefits the next generation.\nL50@P1: Our Blueprint\nL51@P1: Six pillars that policymakers should consider for grounded, technology-aware policies that\nL52@P1-2: empowers and protects youth in the AI era.1. Recognise the positive uses of AI and encourage responsible adoption in education\nL53@P2: AI policy for young people should start from a simple premise: AI can unlock human potential and\nL54@P2: expand how young people learn, create, explore, and prepare for the future. Today's teenagers are\nL55@P2: the first generation coming of age in the Intelligence Age, and they should have access to AI and as\nL56@P2: they prepare to enter the workforce. The goal for policymakers should not be to keep young people\nL57@P2: away from AI, but to ensure they learn to use it safely, critically, creatively, and productively.\nL58@P2: That requires treating AI literacy as both an opportunity and a safety strategy. AI systems are\nL59@P2: meaningfully different from other digital services: tools like ChatGPT for Teens are designed for\nL60@P2: learning and building, not passive scrolling. Today, nearly 9 in 10 teens on ChatGPT use it for\nL61@P2: learning, information, skill-building, or productivity in a single week. Teens need education that\nL62@P2: equips students to critically interpret AI outputs, use AI transparently and effectively, and\nL63@P2: understand both the capabilities and limitations of the technology. They also need parents,\nL64@P2: teachers, and communities with the resources to guide responsible use.\nL65@P2: Not all digital services are the same, and Australian regulation should reflect how AI tools are\nL66@P2: actually used and make room for beneficial learning, productivity, accessibility, and creativity use\nL67@P2: cases to grow responsibly. In schools, for example, adoption should be teacher-led and grounded\nL68@P2: in strong academics. Teachers are irreplaceable and should set the pace for how AI is introduced in\nL69@P2: and around the classroom. Generative AI has great potential to assist teaching and learning and\nL70@P2: reduce the administrative workload in Australian schools, which frees up more time for student\nL71@P2: engagement. As educators build expertise, AI can also support more personalised instruction,\nL72@P2: active learning, tutoring, and support for multilingual learners and other student populations, while\nL73@P2: preserving rigorous instruction in core subjects.\nL74@P2: State and territory governments have primary responsibility for primary and secondary school\nL75@P2: education, including decisions about how to implement national initiatives such as the Australian\nL76@P2: Curriculum. The Productivity Commission has recommended a national approach to educational\nL77@P2: technology and AI tools, to enable lessons learned in the early adopting jurisdictions to be\nL78@P2: incorporated into future investments.\nL79@P2: 2. Identifying teens through privacy preserving age-assurance to treat teens like teens\nL80@P2: and adults like adults\nL81@P2: AI companies should identify teens on their platforms using privacy-protective, risk-based age\nL82@P2: estimation tools to distinguish between teens and adults.\nL83@P2: These tools should minimise the collection of sensitive personal data, while still effectively\nL84@P2: distinguishing users under the age of 18 from adults. Where possible, these methods might also\nL85@P2: rely on operating systems or app stores to determine a user’s age. Age estimation will help AI\nL86@P2-3: companies ensure that they are applying the right protections to the right users. It will facilitateage-appropriate experiences and allow AI companies to treat teens like teens and adults like\nL87@P3: adults.\nL88@P3: OpenAI identifies under-18 Australian users through the same privacy-protective, risk-based age\nL89@P3: prediction approach we use globally. Where age cannot be predicted with sufficient confidence,\nL90@P3: ChatGPT will default to a safer experience.\nL91@P3: Users incorrectly placed in the under-18 experience can quickly confirm their age and restore full\nL92@P3: access by submitting a selfie to Persona, a secure third-party identity-verification service. OpenAI\nL93@P3: does not see the selfie or any identification submitted, and Persona deletes verification data within\nL94@P3: seven days. Consistent with the Privacy Act 1988, we will continue to minimise the personal\nL95@P3: information collected through this process.\nL96@P3: 3. Identifying and mitigating risks to minors through under-18 safety policies.\nL97@P3: Teens have specific developmental needs that differ from adults. AI companies should not cut\nL98@P3: corners on teen safety and wellbeing. AI systems should be designed with age-appropriate\nL99@P3: protections by default.\nL100@P3: AI companies should have safety policies for users under 18 that facilitate age-appropriate outputs\nL101@P3: and evaluate their implementation through testing prior to deployment and through monitoring and\nL102@P3: enforcement after deployment. This includes having clear protocols in place to address serious\nL103@P3: safety situations including self-harm, exploitation, grooming, sexually exploitative content, and other\nL104@P3: high-risk interactions. These should include in-service support, referrals to appropriate resources,\nL105@P3: and timely parental notifications when appropriate. Companies should also design their systems to\nL106@P3: prevent them from generating content that is unsafe or developmentally inappropriate for teens,\nL107@P3: including graphic sexual or violent material.\nL108@P3: To ensure families and users understand the safeguards and tools AI companies have put into\nL109@P3: place, AI companies should seek to publish robust child safety policies that describe the\nL110@P3: company’s approach to protecting children and teens, including the safeguards it implements and\nL111@P3: the parental tools available to families.\nL112@P3: 4. Protect teens from manipulative and deceptive AI outputs\nL113@P3: Teens are still developing emotionally, socially, and cognitively. AI providers should recognise these\nL114@P3: vulnerabilities and design systems that support young people’s wellbeing rather than exploit\nL115@P3: immaturity, trust, or dependency.\nL116@P3: This means moving beyond traditional safety concepts and prescriptive one-size fits all defaults to\nL117@P3: instead address the design underpinning AI outputs. For example, regulation should focus on the\nL118@P3-4: specific proven risks policymakers are seeking to prevent — such as unhealthy emotionaldependency or inappropriate relationship dynamics — and require companies to mitigate those\nL119@P4: risks directly through methods shown to meaningfully mitigate them.\nL120@P4: AI systems should have safeguards in place to prevent outputs that encourage emotional\nL121@P4: overreliance, compulsive engagement, secrecy from parents or trusted adults, or confusion about\nL122@P4: whether someone is speaking with a machine or a human.\nL123@P4: Systems should avoid initiating, reinforcing, or escalating anthropomorphic behaviours that may\nL124@P4: mislead children into perceiving the AI as conscious, emotionally sentient, a romantic partner, or a\nL125@P4: trusted human authority figure.\nL126@P4: AI systems used by teens should also include wellbeing-oriented interventions where relevant. For\nL127@P4: example, systems can encourage breaks during extended sessions, signpost trusted helplines or\nL128@P4: crisis support where distress is detected, and guide users toward offline support from parents,\nL129@P4: guardians, teachers, or professionals when appropriate.\nL130@P4: 5. Developing and deploying a crisis response protocol including embedding features\nL131@P4: that are informed by research and that help people when they need it most.\nL132@P4: AI companies should prioritise teen well-being by developing and deploying documental crisis\nL133@P4: response protocol, and embedding features that support teens’ mental health and wellness. These\nL134@P4: features should be informed by the latest research, guided by external experts, and updated as the\nL135@P4: industry learns more about best practices for facilitating safe experiences for teens using AI. Such\nL136@P4: safeguards should include:\nL137@P4: ● Notifying by default parents who have their account connected to their teen’s account if\nL138@P4: their teen expresses suicidal intent.\nL139@P4: ● Supporting users who express suicidal intent in seeking help and referring them to\n"
